{"id":"n-20261012-o351w2","category":"business","display_title":"De Beers Returns to Israel","display_title_he":"דה בירס חוזרת לישראל","short_summary":"De Beers has resumed operations in Israel after a nine-year absence, coinciding with a new tax agreement designed to enhance the competitive edge of the local diamond industry.","english_level":"C1","words":523,"reading_time":4,"image":null,"sources":[{"name":"Globes","domain":"globes.co.il","url":"https://www.globes.co.il/news/article.aspx?did=1001559157","title":"חברת היהלומים הגדולה דה בירס חוזרת לפעול בישראל","published_at":"2026-10-12T06:59:00.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-12T06:59:00.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-12T07:22:59.683Z","quality_score":9,"interest_score":8,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["After a hiatus of nine years, De Beers, one of the foremost diamond conglomerates globally, has resumed its operations in Israel. According to reports from the Israel Diamond Exchange, upcoming rough diamond tenders will be hosted at the exchange facilities in Ramat Gan, granting local buyers direct access to international merchandise without needing to travel abroad.","This development arrives closely on the heels of a historic tax agreement established between the Israel Diamond Exchange and the local tax authorities, slated to take effect from 2027 to 2028. Under the terms of this new framework, participating diamond professionals will face a predetermined, fixed tax rate calculated as a low percentage of their annual turnover. Furthermore, the previous minimum income floor requirement has been eliminated, removing the obligation for traders to report high baseline earnings during periods of slower business activity. This system offers greater predictability and removes outdated constraints.","Industry advocates emphasize that these regulatory adjustments position Israel advantageously against international trading hubs that already utilize turnover-based taxation models. For instance, Belgium's taxable income for diamond traders sits at a higher percentage of turnover, while Israel maintains zero customs duties on gemstones and jewelry compared to competing global markets like Dubai. Additionally, registering a foreign company in Israel is notably fast for foreign citizens. These collaborative efforts, spearheaded by Diamond Exchange President Nissim Zuaretz alongside Ministry of Economy officials including Foreign Trade Administration head Roee Fisher, aim to revitalize one of the nation's most venerable economic sectors.","Despite these strategic domestic reforms, the broader international diamond market has encountered significant headwinds, partly driven by the growing prevalence of lab-grown diamonds. Statistics for the first half of 2026 indicate that the average price of rough diamonds fell sharply to $105 per carat, marking an approximate thirty-two percent decline relative to the same timeframe in the preceding year. Concurrently, Israeli trading volumes experienced contractions: net exports of polished diamonds dropped by over twenty-two percent to roughly 625.7 million dollars, while net imports of polished diamonds decreased by nearly thirteen percent. Rough diamond imports and exports suffered even steeper contractions during this period.","Traditional primary destinations for Israeli polished diamond exports continued to include the United States, Hong Kong, and Belgium, whereas rough diamond exports primarily targeted India, Belgium, and the United Arab Emirates. Meanwhile, ownership structures surrounding De Beers are also undergoing potential shifts. The company is principally owned by the London-based mining giant Anglo American, which holds an eighty-five percent stake, alongside the government of Botswana. Although a global diamond consortium reached an agreement in July to acquire Anglo American's interest for one billion dollars, the transaction remains pending completion.","De Beers tenders traditionally serve as a primary mechanism for distributing natural rough stones. Prior to submitting bids through the company's digital platform, registered clients engage in a viewing phase to inspect graded lots of stones grouped by size, weight, color, and quality. These auctions welcome registered Sightholders, Non-Sightholders, and other qualified industry participants. Nissim Zuaretz hailed the return of De Beers as a historic milestone moment, characterizing it as a strong endorsement of the Israeli diamond sector, its talented professionals, and its enduring status within the global trade network."],"facts":[{"fact":"De Beers returns to operate in Israel after a nine-year hiatus, holding tenders at the Israel Diamond Exchange in Ramat Gan.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"The return follows a historic tax agreement signed between the Diamond Exchange and the Tax Authority for 2027-2028, setting a fixed, low tax rate as a percentage of turnover and removing minimum income floors.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"In the first half of 2026, the average price of rough diamonds dropped by about 32% to $105 per carat compared to the previous year.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Israel's net exports and imports of both polished and rough diamonds declined in the first half of 2026 compared to the same period in 2025.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"De Beers is owned by London-based Anglo American (85%) and the government of Botswana, though a consortium agreed to buy Anglo American's stake for $1 billion in July, pending completion.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Diamond Exchange President Nissim Zuaretz noted that the return of De Beers is a historic moment and a vote of confidence in the Israeli diamond industry.","sources":[1]}],"glossary":[{"text":"After","lemma":"after","pos":"ADP","he":"אחרי","name":false},{"text":"hiatus","lemma":"hiatus","pos":"NOUN","he":"הפסקה, הפוגה","name":false},{"text":"nine","lemma":"nine","pos":"NUM","he":"תשע","name":false},{"text":"years","lemma":"year","pos":"NOUN